WEBVTT
X-TIMESTAMP-MAP=LOCAL:00:00:00.000,MPEGTS:127826

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.503
Spec-driven development tells the agent how to build what you want.

2
00:00:03.970 --> 00:00:06.072
up front for the project, then for

3
00:00:06.072 --> 00:00:08.174
each feature, getting better as you go.

4
00:00:08.875 --> 00:00:13.079
With SDD, we help the agent with the best quality context.

5
00:00:13.580 --> 00:00:15.782
First with decisions about the project,

6
00:00:15.982 --> 00:00:17.984
then with details about each feature.

7
00:00:18.485 --> 00:00:20.487
This spec is very detailed.

8
00:00:20.687 --> 00:00:24.791
One key skill in SDD is knowing the right level of detail.

9
00:00:25.358 --> 00:00:29.195
If you treat your agent as a highly capable pair programmer,

10
00:00:29.396 --> 00:00:31.231
you'll often hit the right level.

11
00:00:31.831 --> 00:00:34.234
Lots of context about the Goals, mission,

12
00:00:34.634 --> 00:00:36.636
target audience and constraints.

13
00:00:36.736 --> 00:00:39.506
and less about the low-level decisions

14
00:00:39.506 --> 00:00:42.208
the agent can figure out on its own.

15
00:00:42.909 --> 00:00:45.512
What does this SDD workflow look like?

16
00:00:46.112 --> 00:00:48.214
First, we specify the Constitution.

17
00:00:49.015 --> 00:00:52.018
What is the mission, the Tech stack, the Roadmap?

18
00:00:52.719 --> 00:00:57.323
A Constitution is just one way to formalize these project level details.

19
00:00:57.524 --> 00:01:00.126
Many developers use a top-level

20
00:01:00.126 --> 00:01:02.529
agents.md file for this purpose.

21
00:01:02.562 --> 00:01:07.562
But a project Constitution is agent agnostic and more structured.

22
00:01:08.468 --> 00:01:11.004
The Constitution captures the agreement on key

23
00:01:11.004 --> 00:01:13.540
decisions between the human and the agent,

24
00:01:14.040 --> 00:01:16.342
but also the agreement between the humans.

25
00:01:16.643 --> 00:01:18.645
The mission explains the why.

26
00:01:19.145 --> 00:01:22.949
This project's vision, audiences, scope, etc.

27
00:01:23.550 --> 00:01:27.554
Defining these parameters in advance is very common in software projects

28
00:01:27.854 --> 00:01:29.856
and helps guide ongoing decisions.

29
00:01:30.557 --> 00:01:32.559
The tech stack is for the engineering team,

30
00:01:32.859 --> 00:01:37.764
a common understanding of development and deployment technologies and constraints.

31
00:01:38.164 --> 00:01:40.266
The Roadmap is a living document

32
00:01:40.266 --> 00:01:42.368
with a sequence of phases,

33
00:01:42.368 --> 00:01:45.772
each implemented with their own feature spec process.

34
00:01:46.172 --> 00:01:48.675
Once the Constitution has been drafted,

35
00:01:48.675 --> 00:01:51.978
we work on each feature with a repeatable process.

36
00:01:52.545 --> 00:01:55.849
First, plan the feature, implement it,

37
00:01:56.349 --> 00:01:58.451
and finally, validate the result.

38
00:01:59.152 --> 00:02:02.555
In between features, it's time for the Replanning phase.

39
00:02:03.156 --> 00:02:06.126
Revise your Constitution, update the road map,

40
00:02:06.326 --> 00:02:08.428
even improve the process itself.

41
00:02:08.661 --> 00:02:10.396
As you heard a moment ago,

42
00:02:10.396 --> 00:02:12.432
one of the key skills of SDD

43
00:02:12.832 --> 00:02:14.834
is providing the right level of detail

44
00:02:15.235 --> 00:02:18.138
to create the highest quality context.

45
00:02:18.538 --> 00:02:22.942
In the feature phase and the replanning phases, you steer the agent.

46
00:02:23.009 --> 00:02:26.613
Look at it this way. Imagine that you are an architect

47
00:02:26.813 --> 00:02:29.782
and you give detailed drawings of a building to builders.

48
00:02:30.183 --> 00:02:33.586
Then it's up to them. Your role is to design,

49
00:02:34.154 --> 00:02:36.156
supervise the construction,

50
00:02:36.656 --> 00:02:38.658
then review and accept the results,

51
00:02:39.058 --> 00:02:40.660
or ask for changes.

52
00:02:41.060 --> 00:02:44.664
You'll want to avoid telling the builders how to do their jobs

53
00:02:45.064 --> 00:02:48.668
and focus on providing the context they don't know.

54
00:02:49.135 --> 00:02:52.639
Spec-Driven Development gets you from thinking at the start

55
00:02:52.639 --> 00:02:54.741
to delivering at the finish.

56
00:02:55.141 --> 00:02:58.444
Best of all, it keeps you improving from there.

57
00:02:59.145 --> 00:03:01.114
This is one SDD workflow

58
00:03:01.114 --> 00:03:03.216
that modern developers are starting to use.

59
00:03:03.283 --> 00:03:06.786
Soon we'll look at the start, the project constitution.

60
00:03:07.287 --> 00:03:10.056
But first, let's do a little setup preparation.

61
00:03:10.757 --> 00:03:13.259
To get set up with the project repo,

62
00:03:13.359 --> 00:03:16.062
follow instructions in the next reading item.

63
00:03:16.462 --> 00:03:19.265
The following video setup is optional.

64
00:03:19.465 --> 00:03:21.467
If you're already comfortable with setting up

65
00:03:21.467 --> 00:03:23.770
a coding agent like Claude Code

66
00:03:24.070 --> 00:03:26.472
in an IDE such as WebStorm,

67
00:03:26.773 --> 00:03:29.075
feel free to skip this optional video.

68
00:03:29.475 --> 00:03:32.245
See you in the next video or the next one.
